What those thresholds are worth on the scale 05 reports in:
 shift   SIDS   ESSD
   0.1 -0.021 -0.050
   0.2 -0.057 -0.138
   0.3 -0.087 -0.220
   0.4 -0.118 -0.307
   0.6 -0.167 -0.461
   0.8 -0.210 -0.610
  all_other  finds 80% of shifts at or above -0.057 item-score points (ESSD -0.138)
  anchored   finds 80% of shifts at or above -0.054 item-score points (ESSD -0.131)
  lordif     finds 80% of shifts at or above -0.045 item-score points (ESSD -0.108)

Compare against 05, reading its saved output rather than quoting it:
  largest real effect across sex: 4 Low energy at -0.064 item-score points (ESSD -0.105)
  all_other  detection floor -0.057 pts -> the real effect is at or above it
  anchored   detection floor -0.054 pts -> the real effect is at or above it
  lordif     detection floor -0.045 pts -> the real effect is at or above it

An effect below the floor is not a finding, it is a coin flip that landed.
An effect above it is real but still has to be read next to the
false-positive rate in section 1, not instead of it.


=== 4. Contamination: what happens when several items carry DIF ===
05 flags most of the questionnaire under the all-other-item anchor and
blames anchor contamination. Sections 1 and 2 cannot test that claim:
they contain no real DIF, so there is nothing available to contaminate an
anchor with, and both procedures look well behaved there. This section is
the condition that actually tests it. Plant the same shift on a growing
number of items and count flags on the items where nothing was planted.
A procedure with a clean anchor should hold its false-alarm rate flat as
the number of guilty items rises. A contaminated one should not.

